 capture writing honestly; wallet enforcement + interactive override works. John can start using v0 for arbitrary daily work in week 3 (5/30+) â€” no fabricated task thresholds, real-use shapes the data.\n161\t\n162\t### Sprint S2 â€” Real-use accumulation (target 2026-06-05 through 2026-06-18, 2 weeks)\n163\t\n164\t| Theme | Tasks |\n165\t|---|---|\n166\t| Real-use surfaces | Whatever John actually reaches for during S2 â€” projects, research, drafting, finance, code, trip planning. No staged-task framing. Signal capture (task-agnostic JSONL) is the diagnostic for *which* surfaces v0 nailed vs fumbled. |\n167\t| **Per-workflow-type adoption matrix** | Build a matrix from S2's signal data â€” code / research / drafting / finance / trip-planning / project-management â€” report v0 adoption % per type at S2 close. Replaces the binary \"is John using it\" gate (per Pass 1 B-2). |\n168\t| Signal capture (extension) | Add per-skill cost attribution (resource signal â€” extends 5/27 work). Add self-consistency tracking (plan-vs-actual diff using taste output as plan). Promote user-signal interface from terminal one-key to a slash-command (`/approve`, `/redo`, `/edit`) for usability. |\n169\t| **Resume-on-switch (carryon, ~6/8)** | When alpha-v0 (or a v0 specialist) is opened on a second device or second browser tab, the project picker shows each project's last-turn timestamp; on Open, the new terminal pre-loads the last 10 transcript turns into xterm scrollback. No live cross-device sync â€” read-on-open, write-back to same file. ~1 day of work. Files: `AlphaV0ProjectPicker.tsx`, new `app/api/projects-v0/last-turn/route.ts`, `agent-chat-v0.mjs` history-print. Spec: `PLAN-Master-Plan-Update-Specialists-2026-05-16.md` Â§D1. |\n170\t| **v0 specialists (first 2, ~6/8-6/12)** | Author `PLAN-Specialists-v0-Pattern.md` (one-pass design memo: cloning convention, persona-prompt diff strategy, default-project binding semantics, AgentMatrix grouping, demotion UX). Then scaffold **`finance-alpha`** (skill bundle: `family-accountant`, `project-manager`, `use-claude-code`; default project: `family-finances`) and **`code-alpha`** (skill bundle: `expert-coder`, `expert-coder-Win11`, `project-manager`, `deep-think`, `use-claude-code`; default project: `gwl-v0-build`). Both inherit alpha-v0's t2r-dispatcher path, $100/mo per-agent wallet, signal capture. AgentMatrix `V0_SPECIALIST_IDS` allowlist gets the two ids; the new \"v0 Specialists\" sub-group renders. Persona files at `Sandbox Setup Package\\PERSONAS\\global\\<id>-personal-manager.md`. Routing entries cloned from alpha-v0. End-to-end smoke for both. Spec: `PLAN-Master-Plan-Update-Specialists-2026-05-16.md` Â§B. |\n171\t| Sleep loop (agent-layer) | Implement first sleep mutation: skill success-rate scoring with Wilson intervals; **demote skills below threshold by surfacing for John's approval â€” no auto-retire** (per Pass 1 Q-A; aligned with GBrain \"human-stays-in-the-loop\" philosophy). |\n172\t| **GBrain operational wallet audit** | Per B-12 â€” audit GBrain's actual API spend (LLM dream-cycle calls, embedding API, classifier fail-improve loops) on real-use data. Right-size the GBrain wallet from the $30/mo starting estimate. |\n173\t| Wallet enforcement (extension) | Per-agent monthly burn dashboard. Per-skill cost attribution surfaces top-cost skills. Now scoped per (agent Ã— month) which includes alpha-v0 + each specialist independently. |\n174\t| Specialist tile-collapse decision | At S2 close, decide: keep v0 specialists as their own AgentMatrix sub-row (current default), or collapse them into the alpha-v0 tile with project-bound auto-specialist-pick? Default recommendation: keep them as sibling tiles in S2; reconsider at S3 close. |\n175\t| Cleanup | Resolve project-manager-kit canonical divergence (if not done in S1). Audit alpha-v0 + first specialists' personas against actual usage patterns from S2 signal data (validation, not redesign). |\n176\t| Cadence-sync | Weekly entries in `logs/cadence-sync/` â€” the new fourth bullet (Specialist track state per Â§12) starts being meaningful in week 2 once finance-alpha + code-alpha are running. |\n177\t\n178\t**S2 success criterion:** Per-workflow-type adoption matrix produced from real signal data. Numerical answer per type, not a vibe. Green = 3+ workflow types showing â‰¥50% v0 adoption with stable signal quality. If S2 review finds v0 fell short on most types, S3 reshapes per Â§11 contingencies.\n179\t\n180\t### Sprint S3 â€” Polish + brain-track resumption (target 2026-06-19 through 2026-07-02, 2 weeks)\n181\t\n182\t| Theme | Tasks |\n183\t|---|---|\n184\t| **Bridge expansion** | Claude Code Bridge is LIVE since 2026-05-14. S3 expands bridge use cases: install/upgrade automation via the bridge; log collection; host-state probes; expert-coder-Win11 pre-flight runs against real host data. |\n185\t| **v0 polish + missing-skill identification** | Read S2 signal data, identify the skills v0 lacks for John's actual workflows, install missing skills from marketplaces or author new ones via `skillify`. Mature the skill-demotion UX (still human-in-loop; auto-retire stays v1+). Both \"second v0-shaped agent\" and \"auto-retire\" dropped per Pass 1 Q-A + Q-B AMENDs. |\n186\t| **v0 specialists (next 2-4)** | Read S2 per-workflow-type adoption matrix and pick 2-4 specialists from the workflow types showing clear v0 fumble (taste-call retrieval pulling wrong notes, reasoning-pass missing project-specific context, etc.). Candidate pool: `math-research-alpha`, `teaching-alpha`, `drafting-alpha`, `trip-planning-alpha`. **Do not pre-commit which 2-4** â€” decide at start of S3 from S2 data; ship in S3 weeks 1-2. Per-specialist construction template established in S2 (`PLAN-Specialists-v0-Pattern.md`); marginal cost ~1 day each. |\n187\t| **Specialist demotion** | If a S2 specialist (`finance-alpha` or `code-alpha`) shows lower per-turn quality or lower John-use rate than generalist alpha-v0 on its own workflow type, flag for retirement and surface for John's approval. Auto-retire stays human-in-loop through v0 per master plan Â§7 Q-A. |\n188\t| Brain layer (Brain Wave 8) | Resume Phase C Step A. Llama-3.1-8B QLoRA on gwl-server 4070, training on the now-rich adjudicator-tuple corpus (including v0 t2r contributions from S1-S2). This is *brain-layer work* running parallel with Sprint S3 agent-layer work. |\n189\t| **Promote-to-v1 gate evaluation** | At S3 close: evaluate per-workflow-type adoption matrix accumulated across S2 + S3. If green per Â§3 promote criterion, scope S4 = v1 cognitive cells. If not green, scope S4 per Â§11 failure-mode branch. |\n190\t| Cadence-sync | Weekly entries; closing entry summarizes Sprint + Brain Wave dependencies for S4. |\n191\t\n192\t**S3 success criterion:** Per-workflow-type matrix shows green (3+ types â‰¥50% adoption) â€” promote to v1 scope. OR red â€” invoke Â§11 branch (a/b/c). OR mixed â€” S4 = polish + targeted patch. Brain side: Phase C Step A green smoke (Llama-3.1-8B QLoRA training loop runs on the 4070 against the corpus).\n193\t\n194\t### Promote-to-v1 gate\n195\t\n196\tAfter S3 closes, evaluate against the v0 â†’ v1 promotion gate (table Â§3): per-workflow-type adoption matrix with â‰¥3 workflow types at â‰¥50% v0 adoption + stable signal quality + architecture demonstrably stable across real tasks. If green: scope S4 as v1 cognitive-cell substrate. If mixed (1-2 types working, others not): S4 = targeted patch + delay v1 by one sprint. If red (broad failure): invoke Â§11 failure-mode contingency â€” rethink experiment branches (a)/(b)/(c).\n197\t\n198\t---\n199\t\n200\t## 5. Parallel workstreams (where v4 work continues)\n201\t\n202\t**Status:** locked from STATE-OF-PROJECT + retro\n203\t\n204\tv4's brain work does not stop because v5 reorients. It continues *in parallel* with v0 build, on a different cadence (Brain Waves alongside Agent Sprints).\n205\t\n206\t| Workstream | v4 origin | v5 disposition | Cadence |\n207\t|---|---|---|---|\n208\t| Adjudicator-training-tuple corpus capture | Wave 7 Phase 2 (G-32) | **Continues** â€” this is the input to v2 and beyond. Already accumulating since 2026-05-08. | Runs continuously; no sprint allocation. |\n209\t| Tier-3 distillation losses (G-26) | Wave 7 Phase 2 | **Continues** â€” distillation is needed for v2 local students. | Wired; runs on tuple ingestion. |\n210\t| Replay scheduler (G-29) | Wave 7 Phase 2 | **Continues** â€” feeds the in-harness brain-training pipeline. | Wired; runs on tuple ingestion. |\n211\t| Drift detection (G-31 drift-judge expert, Tier-1 watcher) | Wave 7 Phase 2-3 | **Continues** â€” drift dissipates via replay-scheduler boost, no auto-school flip. | Wired; runs continuously. |\n212\t| G-28 skill-evolution enable | Wave 7 Phase 3 (gated off) | **Stays gated off in v5 until S3 at earliest**, then flip if the agent-layer skill-scoring is stable. | Sprint S3+ candidate. |\n213\t| CURR-2 (Gemini gap-fill loop) | v4 Â§6.1 | **Defers** to S4+ â€” agent-layer work is the higher priority. The 5/7 stubs and gaps stay logged. | Not in v0 critical path. |\n214\t| CURR-3 (curation pass) | v4 Â§6.1 | **Defers** to S4+ for the same reason. | Not in v0 critical path. |\n215\t| Phase B Windows smoke | v4 Phase B | **Continues opportunistically** â€” happens when v0 build needs a Windows-smoke confirmation anyway. | Sprint S1-2 ambient. |\n216\t| Phase B first GLCI cadence (post-baseline) | v4 Phase B | **Continues opportunistically** â€” alpha-linear/alpha-lorentz keep their research role. | Sprint S2+. |\n217\t| Plasticity Session 8+ (validity rules, shadow-evaluator, proposal store, dashboard) | v4 / Session-7 roadmap | **Continues** with v4's cadence; not gated on v5. | Brain-Wave 8 (separate track from Sprint cadence). |\n218\t| Phase C Step A (Llama-3.1-8B install) | Authored 2026-05-13, paused | **Resumes in S3** â€” concurrent with v0 trust accumulation. | Sprint S3 brain-layer slot. |\n219\t| Phase C Step B (Tier-3 LoRA wiring) | v4 Phase C | **Sprint S4+** â€” gated on Step A. | Sprint S4. |\n220\t| Punch-list infrastructure (Caddy install, LSASS ASR re-flip, ASR-loop bug in Setup-GWL-Server.ps1, second 32 GB DIMM) | STATE-OF-PROJECT Â§C | **Opportunistic** â€” pulled in when it blocks a sprint task; otherwise on the punch list. | Background. |\n221\t\n222\tThe brain-layer corpus accumulation is *invisible to John day-to-day* but is the substrate that v1 and v2 train on. **v0 producing real interactions is what makes v1 and v2 possible** â€” every v0 turn captured in JSONL is a future training pair.\n223\t\n224\t---\n225\t\n226\t## 6. The hooks meta-architecture\n227\t\n228\t**Status:** master (the hook surface is locked; the per-hook implementation is sprint work)\n229\t\n230\tPer Retro Entry 16, six hooks are load-bearing for v0 â€” the points in the agent loop where GWL's quality-signal capture latches into GBrain's runtime. These are not GWL inventions; they are the standard agent-runtime hook surface that LangGraph, Letta, AutoGen, and Claude Code itself expose. GWL's contribution is *what gets written to JSONL at each*.\n231\t\n232\t| Hook | What v0 writes there | What sleep does with it |\n233\t|---|---|---|\n234\t| `session_start` | Load relevant episodic memory for project; check tool availability; load `.pm/state.json`. | Update session-frequency stats per project. |\n235\t| `pre_turn` | Retrieval over skill library; surface \"you did this before\" candidates; inject quality-scored skills into context (taste call). **Replay signal: extends GBrain's `eval_candidates` capture path** (with `GBRAIN_CONTRIBUTOR_MODE=1`, native since GBrain v0.25.0) rather than reimplementing â€” v5 inherits the retrieval-capture work GBrain already did. | Adjust similarity threshold for reuse-proposal (Retro Entry 16: \"hook thresholds become learnable parameters\"). |\n236\t| `pre_tool_call` | Cost check (resource signal); confirm tool selection; possibly route to cheaper model. | Update tool-success stats per (task-type, tool). |\n237\t| `post_tool_call` | Log outcome (resource signal: time/tokens); update skill score if tool was associated with a named skill. | Demote skills with low success rates; surface for retire. |\n238\t| `post_turn` | The 5-flavor JSONL row: input, taste-output, reasoning-output, skills_used, tools_used, latency, cost, optional outcome-verifier result. | Tier-1 maintenance digest; Tier-3 brain-training pair if criteria met. |\n239\t| `on_user_feedback` | User signal: approve / edit / reject / redo / silence-as-implicit-approve. | Back-propagate to skill scores; raise envelope for next sleep cycle. |\n240\t| `on_error` | Capture error; route to fallback; surface to user with context. | Pattern-match errors over time; propose a `fix-it` skill via skillify. |\n241\t| `session_end` | Episodic â†’ semantic consolidation; append session summary; refresh investor brief if stale. | Tier-2 consolidation step. |\n242\t| `sleep_start` / `sleep_step` / `sleep_end` | Tier-1/2/3 sleep cycle entries (these already exist on the brain side from Wave 7). | This is where the agent learning actually happens â€” hook-threshold adjustments, skill score updates, hook-routing changes. |\n243\t\n244\tThe **load-bearing insight from Retro Entry 16** is that *hooks themselves are learnable*. The `pre_turn` similarity threshold for \"this is similar enough to reuse\" is a number. Sleep adjusts it based on outcomes. The agent learns its routing without retraining any model. This is the cheapest learning mechanism in the entire stack and is gross-margin-positive on day one of v0.\n245\t\n246\t---\n247\t\n248\t## 7. Open questions â€” fully resolved across Pass 1 + Pass 2\n249\t\n250\t**Status:** locked\n251\t\n252\tAll drafting-time questions Q1-Q7 are walkthrough-locked with John (see Â§3). The critique passes added Q-A through Q-N and resolved each:\n253\t\n254\t- **Q-A.** *Auto-retire timing.* AMEND â€” human-in-loop demotion through all of v0; auto-retire is v1+ capability when brain-side cell substrate adds signal. Aligns with GBrain's own \"human-stays-in-the-loop\" philosophy (Pass 2 B-17).\n255\t- **Q-B.** *Second v0-shaped agent in S3.* AMEND â€” strike from S3 themes. Specialization happens *after* v0 lands (Retro Entry 15); S3 is when we find out if v0 works. Replaced with \"v0 polish + missing-skill identification from signal data.\"\n256\t- ~~Q-C, Q-D, Q-I~~ obsolesced by walkthrough (wallet sizing locked at $100/$80; staged trust-ladder dropped).\n257\t- **Q-E.** *Bun-only runtime readiness.* AMEND â€” escalated to dedicated S1 day-4 task (winget install + version pin + Bootstrap-Agentic-Home.ps1 update + CLAUDE.md scope note \"use Bun only for GBrain operations\").\n258\t- **Q-F.** *GBrain resolver contract.* KEEP-amended â€” Pass 2 research confirmed GBrain v0.19+ natively supports `AGENTS.md` at workspace root. Bridge reduces to ~10 lines + 1 env var (`OPENCLAW_WORKSPACE=C:\\dev\\Agentic-Home`). S1 day-7 task.\n259\t- **Q-G.** *Cross-provider routing schema.* AMEND â€” schema extension to `routing.json` for per-half model fields is **load-bearing for t2r** and lands S1 day-8 regardless of cross-provider exercise. Opus-cross-wallet plumbing defers to S2.\n260\t- **Q-H.** *Capture envelope format.* AMEND-significantly â€” Pass 2 read `adjudicator-tuple-writer.mjs`; envelope's `brain` field already takes `'taste'|'reasoning'`. v0 t2r writes G-27 envelope natively with `source: 'v0-t2r'` annotation + `mode: 'practice'`. Triggers Pass 2 B-11 namespace finding.\n261\t- **Q-J.** *Letta revisit.* KEEP-amended â€” sharper trigger: revisit only on (a) Pass 2 explicitly identifying GBrain memory as load-bearing failure, OR (b) S1 GBrain install spike failing. Pass 2 did not identify failure; trigger is dormant.\n262\t- **Q-K.** *Graceful-degrade on cap-hit.* AMEND â€” specced into S1 day-14 wallet enforcement: refuse new taste call + preserve in-flight reasoning + **interactive cap-override** (\"approve $X overage for this turn?\") + logged rationale.\n263\t- **Q-L.** *Post-finalization artifacts.* KEEP â€” confirmed Pass 2. Subplan-per-sprint (`plans/subplans/S<n>.md`) + `LIVING-GAPS.md` (or section of this file). Author once the first execution-time gap surfaces.\n264\t- **Q-M.** *Three-`gbrain`-paths disambiguation.* AMEND-major (Pass 2 B-11) â€” split rename: `C:\\dev\\Agentic-Home\\gbrain\\` â†’ `gwl-runtime\\`; `C:\\Users\\john\\gbrain\\` â†’ `gwl-corpus\\` (30+ brain-code files); Tan's GBrain takes workspace-root `gbrain/` post-install. Spec memo `PATH-RENAME-design.md` then execute, S1 days 2-3. Test gate stays green post-rename.\n265\t- **Q-N.** *v0 t2r envelope downstream-feed.* AMEND â€” v0 t2r writes G-27 envelope with `source: 'v0-t2r'` annotation; downstream Tier-3 distillation queue + corpus readers filter by source. No format change; just an annotation extension.\n266\t\n267\tAll critique items resolved. Bounded-iteration discipline holds (Pass 1 + Pass 2 only).\n268\t\n269\t---\n270\t\n271\t## 8. Risk register\n272\t\n273\t**Status:** master\n274\t\n275\t| Risk | Likelihood | Impact | Mitigation |\n276\t|---|---|---|---|\n277\t| GBrain proves harder to install / less stable than the 30-minute claim suggests | Medium | High (delays S1) | Spike GBrain install on a sacrificial subdirectory in S1 day 4; bail if green-smoke takes >1 day. **Vendor a known-good snapshot** of GBrain at workspace level so future upgrades are opt-in (Pass 1 B-1). Fallback: Letta per Q-J trigger. |\n278\t| GBrain upstream stalls or pivots in S2-S5 timeframe (bus-factor â€” single author, 5-week-old project) | Medium | Medium | Vendored snapshot per above. Plan B mental experiment: list components we'd have to write ourselves if GBrain disappeared â€” queue as Pass-2-deferred work post-S1. Ecosystem has 3+ third-party tools building on GBrain (per Pass 2 research), so bus factor is distributed. |\n279\t| Bun-as-required-runtime conflicts with existing Node/npm convention | Medium | Medium | Bun installs side-by-side with Node; isolated scope: use Bun *only* for GBrain operations, document in CLAUDE.md, integrate into Bootstrap-Agentic-Home.ps1. |\n280\t| **B-11 namespace rename touches 30+ brain-code files; test-gate could break** | Medium | High | Scripted find-and-replace `'gbrain'` â†’ `'gwl-corpus'`; full 498/498 test-suite re-run as the verify gate; rollback path is a `git revert` since rename lands in its own commit. ASR-loop bug in Setup-GWL-Server.ps1 may interact; verify before scheduling. |\n281\t| t2r dispatcher latency (~2Ã— single-call) feels too slow for daily use | Low-Medium | High (kills John's adoption) | Top-tier-both is the v0 architectural commitment (Q2 walkthrough) â€” latency is a *measured* risk, not a tunable parameter; if too slow, S2 review captures it from real-use signal data and Â§11 contingency (b) provides the experiment branch. |\n282\t| Cross-provider plumbing (Opus escalation crosses wallets) breaks `routing.json` or wallet enforcement | Low | Medium | Default config (Gemini Pro both) is single-provider; cross-provider only on Opus-escalation path which can be implemented after S1 ships. Schema extension lands S1 day-8; cross-provider exercising deferred to S2. |\n283\t| $100/mo t2r wallet cap proves too tight even at Pro-both | Medium | Medium | Q1-Q2 precedence rule: raise the wallet, don't downgrade models. First wallet adjustment is *expected*, not failure (B-13). Revision triggers: first soft-alert hit, or end-of-S1 review, or John reports cap-friction. |\n284\t| **GBrain's own LLM+embedding spend not in the $100 t2r wallet; could surprise on first month** (B-12) | Medium | Low-Medium | Separate $30/mo starting estimate for GBrain operational wallet; audit task in S2 right-sizes against real-use data; track on its own line in `logs/wallet-revisions.jsonl`. Confusion-avoidance: cap-hit on this line doesn't mean v0 t2r failure. |\n285\t| v0 quality-signal capture overhead degrades agent responsiveness | Low | Medium | Async writes; fail-soft (signal write failure does not block the turn). |\n286\t| Phase C Step A (Llama-3.1-8B install) blocks on HF account / Llama agreement / HF_TOKEN | Medium | Low (deferred to S3 anyway) | John handles HF account when convenient; S3 has slack. |\n287\t| Memory `feedback_powershell_51_compat` is incomplete (gwl-server runs PS 5.1; some sprint scripts may want PS 7 for newer cmdlets) | Low | Low | `expert-coder-Win11` skill (shipped 2026-05-14) handles this. |\n288\t| Coworkâ†”host bridge already live (2026-05-14) â€” does that change risk profile? | Low | Low-positive | Reduces script-emit risk; enables direct probes during sprints. Net: tooling improvement. |\n289\t| Alpha persona file drifts from v0 reality | Mitigated | Low | S1 day-1/2 `PERSONA-DESIGN-Alpha-v0.md` memo + 5/26 rewrite; S2 audit confirms. |\n290\t\n291\t---\n292\t\n293\t## 9. Definition of done â€” v5 plan ratification\n294\t\n295\t**Status:** met\n296\t\n297\tThis plan is \"done\" per the SESSION-HANDOFF-V5 definition-of-done checklist. All items shipped:\n298\t\n299\t- [x] Current-state recap drawing on STATE-OF-PROJECT + Wave 7 close + Phase C scripts + today's tooling adds (Â§2)\n300\t- [x] Explicit v4 â†’ v5 reframe acknowledged (brainâ‰ agent; agent-layer commoditized; GBrain adoption; quality-signal hooks) (Â§1)\n301\t- [x] v0/v1/v2 spec table with brain layer + agent layer + sleep updates + wallet + success criterion (Â§3)\n302\t- [x] v0 build plan â€” concrete, dated, decomposed into sprint sessions; days-to-weeks not months (Â§4)\n303\t- [x] Parallel workstreams from v4 explicitly disposed (continue / defer / supersede) (Â§5)\n304\t- [x] Refreshed gaps analysis as a sibling file ([GAPS-2026-05-14.md](./GAPS-2026-05-14.md)) â€” frozen at master-promotion\n305\t- [x] Critique-ready section markers â€” every Â§ has a `Status:` line per project-manager skill discipline\n306\t- [x] Two bounded critique passes complete (Pass 1 + Pass 2); no Pass 3\n307\t- [x] Risk register populated (Â§8)\n308\t- [x] Failure-mode contingencies named (Â§11)\n309\t- [x] Cadence-sync mechanism defined (Â§12)\n310\t\n311\t---\n312\t\n313\t## 10. What's explicitly *not* in v5\n314\t\n315\t**Status:** locked from SESSION-HANDOFF \"parked\" list\n316\t\n317\tItems that v5 acknowledges as designed-and-deferred, not abandoned. Adding any of these to v5's critical path requires John's explicit approval.\n318\t\n319\t- Full plasticity catalog beyond first-plasticity (post-v2 future work)\n320\t- FBB / PVB+P / Vulcan-Intuitionalist hemispheric work\n321\t- Multiple brain architectures (each separately would warrant their own v5-analog plan)\n322\t- Full K-12 curriculum run across two pilots (v4 Phase D)\n323\t- Multi-cluster specialization (W / S / Math agents â€” the v4 cohort-spawning framing with manager agents) is **RE-OPENED** as of 2026-05-21 (Alpha-b0v1 phase). \n324\t- **The Alpha-b0v1 Organization:** We will implement a hierarchical multi-agent system with:\n325\t  1. **Ecgberht (Executive PA):** Global router and master interface.\n326\t  2. **Per-Project PMs:** Dedicated agents (e.g., `pm-teaching`) with domain-isolated memory and skills.\n327\t  3. **Specialist Clones:** Dynamic branching of code/finance/etc specialists bound to specific projects.\n328\t  4. **Nightly Consolidation:** A \"Librarian\" loop to harvest and share learning globally.\n329\t- **Wallet Guard:** This phase begins as a **Low-Volume Pilot**. Per-agent wallets are tiered ($30/$100/$5) and child clones inherit the parent's budget.\n330\t- INFRA-2 (web admin portal)\n331\t- INFRA-3 (mobile + voice)\n332\t- Tournament 2 / 3 (Tournament 1 v2 is gated on Phase B operational data, which is unblocked but not prioritized)\n333\t- Hemispheric ablation study\n334\t- Letta â€” superseded by GBrain's retrieval; revisit only per Q-J sharpened trigger\n335\t- Build-vs-buy on OpenClaw â€” John has chosen build/use; flagged for revisit if engineering cost compounds\n336\t- **Forking GBrain (Plan B contingency, post-v0)** â€” if GBrain upstream stalls or develops unfixable bugs, vendor a known-good snapshot and maintain locally. Triggered by S2/S3 findings, not by default.\n337\t\n338\t---\n339\t\n340\t## 11. Failure-mode contingencies\n341\t\n342\t**Status:** locked from Pass 1 B-5 + Pass 2 amendment plan\n343\t\n344\tThe Q5 walkthrough decision (\"if v0 broadly doesn't work, we rethink the architecture\") needs concrete branches. Three contingencies are pre-named so the rethink starts from a position of context, not a blank page.\n345\t\n346\t**Triggered by:** S3 promote-gate evaluation against the per-workflow-type adoption matrix (Â§3, Â§4 S3).\n347\t\n348\t### Branch (a) â€” v0 works on most workflow types\n349\t\n350\tPer-workflow-type matrix shows â‰¥3 types green. **Proceed to v1 as drafted.** S4 scopes v1 cognitive-cell substrate.\n351\t\n352\t### Branch (b) â€” v0 works on some, fails on others\n353\t\n354\tMatrix shows 1-2 types green, others red. S4 = targeted patch via skill/tool/prompt iteration in the failing workflow types. **Do not promote to v1** until all critical types green. Specific patches per workflow type are spec'd from the signal data (which skill, which tool, which prompt segment of t2r).\n355\t\n356\t### Branch (c) â€” v0 broadly fails\n357\t\n358\tMatrix shows 0-1 types green across the board. S4 = \"rethink experiment.\" Three named candidate experiments to run (one per S4 sub-sprint or in parallel if resourceable):\n359\t\n360\t1. **Architecture experiment.** Swap t2r split for single-call (no taste/reasoning bifurcation) with thinner prompts on the same agent stack. Measure delta. Confirms whether t2r adds value vs raw single-frontier-call.\n361\t2. **Model experiment.** Try Anthropic Opus on both halves (after raising the wallet per Q1-Q2 precedence). Measures quality lift from model substitution at top tier.\n362\t3. **Runtime experiment.** Try Cowork's MCP integration with GBrain bolted in, *instead of* OpenClaw+GBrain. Lower switching cost than redesigning architecture; tests whether OpenClaw's runtime is contributing failure vs the t2r design.\n363\t\n364\tThe plan should never be a single thread. Each branch has a named first experiment ready to run.\n365\t\n366\t---"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19c01179-cb27-4592-b8ab-2eca4f61db86","uuid":"2408cb58-5c54-457a-a0d2-0ed5f578b02c","timestamp":"2026-06-22T18:46:10.931Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\Agentic-Home\\MASTER-PLAN-v5.md","content":"# Master Rollout Plan v5 â€” Agent-First and the v0â€“v1â€“v2 Ladder\n\n**Date:** 2026-05-14\n**Status:** **MASTER** (living document). Synthesized from two parallel Cowork sessions that independently produced v5 drafts on 2026-05-14; the more-developed post-Pass-2 draft forms ~95% of this substrate, with surgical improvements pulled from the leaner proto. Further refinement happens *during execution* and is surfaced via `LIVING-GAPS.md` (created when the first execution-time gap surfaces) or direct edits to this file.\n**Bounded-iteration discipline holds: no Pass 3.** Two critique passes (2026-05-14 morning) are the load-bearing review. Execution teaches the rest.\n**Supersedes:** [Master Rollout Plan v4 â€” Pilots, Plasticity, and the Path to Production (May 5 2026)](./Master%20Rollout%20Plan%20v4%20â€”%20Pilots,%20Plasticity,%20and%20the%20Path%20to%20Production%20%28May%205%202026%29.md) (v4.2 addendum 2026-05-08).\n**Companion:** [GAPS-2026-05-14.md](./GAPS-2026-05-14.md) â€” what v5 keeps / drops / defers / supersedes from v4. Frozen at master-promotion; emerging gaps go to `LIVING-GAPS.md`.\n**Critique provenance:** [CRITIQUE-PASS-1-v5-proto.md](./CRITIQUE-PASS-1-v5-proto.md), [CRITIQUE-PASS-2-v5-proto.md](./CRITIQUE-PASS-2-v5-proto.md).\n**Archived source drafts:** [archive/v5-drafts/MASTER-PLAN-v5--parallel-session-post-Pass2.md](./archive/v5-drafts/MASTER-PLAN-v5--parallel-session-post-Pass2.md) (the post-Pass-2 draft, ~95% of this content) and [archive/v5-drafts/PLAN-Master-Rollout-v5-proto-2026-05-14--session-A.md](./archive/v5-drafts/PLAN-Master-Rollout-v5-proto-2026-05-14--session-A.md) (the leaner proto, retained for the few framing improvements pulled forward).\n**Inputs consumed:**\n- [SESSION-HANDOFF-V5-Plan-Draft-2026-05-13.md](./SESSION-HANDOFF-V5-Plan-Draft-2026-05-13.md) â€” 18 locked decisions, parked list, definition of done\n- [V5-RETRO-Brainstorm-2026-05-13.md](./V5-RETRO-Brainstorm-2026-05-13.md) â€” 26 entries of John+Claude dialogue that derived the locked decisions\n- [STATE-OF-PROJECT-2026-05-13.md](./STATE-OF-PROJECT-2026-05-13.md) â€” v4-state ledger\n- [PLAN-expert-coder-Win11-2026-05-13.md](./PLAN-expert-coder-Win11-2026-05-13.md) â†’ realized as the `expert-coder-Win11` skill, 2026-05-14\n- [CLAUDE.md](./CLAUDE.md) â€” workspace orientation\n- Workspace memory (`feedback_powershell_51_compat`, `gwl_server_hardware`, `skills_live_in_dev`, `feedback_terse_status`)\n\n---\n\n## Versioning convention (canonical â€” see [VERSIONING.md](./VERSIONING.md))\n\nThe GWL **system version** is `v<B>.<H>`: **B** = t2r **brain** generation, **H** = agentic **harness/capacity** generation (OpenClaw + G-Brain + Helping Hand/Aurora). **Current: `v2.1`** = gen-2 dynamic-context t2r brain (implemented + live, verified 2026-05-24/25) + gen-1 harness.\n\n- Build iterations *within* a generation are tracked by date+commit, **not** by a version digit. Earlier \"v2.2 / v2.3 / v2.5\" labels were gen-2 **brain build** iterations mislabeled as system bumps â€” corrected to: the system is `v2.1`, the brain is gen-2.\n- This is **orthogonal** to the **v0â€“v1â€“v2 agent-rollout ladder** in this plan's title (the ladder = agent/deliverable maturity stage). The agent `alpha-v0` is a **v0-ladder vehicle** that runs the **`v2.1` system**. Agent id â‰  system version.\n\n## Headline\n\n**v4 was brain-first. v5 is agent-first.** The brain layer is GWL's moat and is in good enough shape to start v0 today; the agent layer is commoditized and we're going to borrow Tan's GBrain wholesale. **v0's explicit goal is to replace Cowork for daily work** â€” Alpha as the v0 vehicle, t2r dispatcher (taste â†’ reasoning, both halves top-tier frontier), GBrain underneath, quality-signal hooks at every seam. Days-to-weeks to v0, not months.\n\n---\n\n## Update â€” 2026-05-27: t2r cost diagnosis â†’ Sovereign t2r Harness convergence\n\n**Status:** new direction locked; software plan next. Supersedes the v5 headline's \"borrow Tan's GBrain *wholesale* at the agent layer\" â€” see the reframe at the end of this block.\n\nA week-long cost problem on the daily-use agents (Ecgberht turns appearing to cost $0.45â€“$10.11) was root-caused and the path forward converged across three multi-round, multi-agent debates + OpenClaw SDK ground-truth spikes + cost simulations. Findings (all verified, not asserted):\n\n- **The cost meter was lying, not the brain.** The gateway client priced every turn at the configured fallback model (Sonnet) and ignored the cache split, overstating cost **3â€“10Ã—** (the \"$10.11\" turn really cost ~$1.07). MoE tiering AND prompt caching already work on the gateway (ledger: 85kâ€“1.98M cache_read tokens). **Fixed + live-verified** (truthful per-turn cost, real model/tier, cache-hit%); see `PLAN-Reporting-Accuracy-2026-05-27.md`.\n- **The real cost/latency driver is the within-turn agentic tool-loop**, which OpenClaw cannot tier per step (it fixes one model per run) and whose framework overhead (~8.6s warm; auth + per-turn tool-registry rebuild) is large. SDK spikes: no stable `executeTool` (OpenClaw is an MCP *client*); `subagent.run` is the only stable way to reuse OpenClaw's stateful tools; `ownsCompaction:true` fires per-iteration compaction on the gateway path.\n- **Convergence (mine + two external efforts):** build a **Sovereign t2r Harness** â€” a custom loop the agent fully owns, running a **recursive t2r cycle at every ReAct step** (the validated gold standard, `GOLD-STANDARD-T2R-Recursive-Agentic-Loop.md`). **Key correction:** do NOT swap *remote* reasoning models mid-loop (cross-model cache cold-start = measured **+120â€“242%**); win instead via free-local observation **compression** + **local sub-decisions ($0)** + **one cache-stable remote model per loop**.\n- **Final spec:** `REPORT-Sovereign-t2r-Harness-Gold-Standard-FINAL.md` â€” per-agent **Soul** (cache-stable file), **persistent 3-tier memory** (episodic/semantic/procedural, written by free local models, retrieved into the message tail), **tiered tools** (basic in-harness via off-the-shelf MCP; complex/stateful delegated to OpenClaw via `subagent.run`, migrated in over time).\n- **G-Brain reframe (the strategy change vs the v5 headline):** GBrain is installed but is **demoted from \"the agent layer\" to a deferred, offline training flywheel** that distills the harness's own cheap local tier from the `g27-v3` envelopes the harness emits â€” **never in the per-turn hot loop** (Bun-leak rule). v5 planned to borrow GBrain wholesale *as* the agent layer; the convergence builds our own sovereign agent layer instead and keeps GBrain for what it's uniquely good at (offline LoRA/distillation).\n\n**Decision:** go directly to Phase 2 (build the Sovereign t2r Harness). Next artifact: the module-by-module software plan. Session log: `SESSION-LOG-2026-05-27-t2r-harness-convergence.md`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The v4 â†’ v5 reframe (load-bearing)\n\n**Status:** locked\n\nv4 mostly designed a *brain*. The tasteâ†’reasoning architecture, the cognitive cells, the plasticity catalog (26 mutations across 5 layers â€” not v4's prose-claimed 17), the curriculum-driven training, the adjudicator and its training tuples, the distillation losses, the drift detection â€” all of it is *cognition substrate*. v4 calls this work \"the path to production\" but production of *what* was hand-waved: the same plan also gestures at clusters of W/S/Math agents using the brain, but never separated the agent layer from the brain layer.\n\n**The single deepest cut in this review (Retro Entry 1 â†’ Entry 14):** brain â‰  agent. The agent is the persistent operating unit that *uses* a brain to do work â€” it has its own memory, skills, tools, planning loop, quality-signal pipeline, and ongoing identity. v4 fused the two; v5 pries them apart.\n\nThree corollaries flow from the reframe:\n\n1. **The agent layer is commoditized; the GWL brain is the moat.** Anthropic, Google, OpenAI, and a dozen frameworks (LangGraph, Letta, CrewAI, AutoGen, Cowork, Claude Code) have collectively spent hundreds of engineer-years on the agent layer. GWL needs *best-in-class* at this layer but does not need to be *innovative* here. The tasteâ†’reasoning brain, the cognitive cell substrate, the in-harness brain-training pipeline â€” those are differentiated and worth defending. Everything else: borrow.\n2. **GBrain (Garry Tan's, MIT, 11.1kâ˜…, OpenClaw-native, released 2026-04-05) delivers ~80% of v0's agent-layer scope** in a 30-minute install. 29 baseline skills + Git-versioned markdown Brain Repo + pgvector retrieval + dream cycle + entity-tier escalation + `skillify` workflow + `Minions` job queue + MCP server. GBrain is purpose-built for the runtime John has chosen and is open-source under terms compatible with this workspace. **Adopting GBrain replaces months of v4-implied build-effort at the agent layer.**\n3. **GBrain does NOT deliver the quality-signal capture loop.** No skill success-rate scoring, no Wilson-CI tournaments at the agent layer, no RLHF/DPO, no 5-flavor signals (outcome / user / resource / self-consistency / replay). This is *the* v5 GWL addition: hook-layer instrumentation at GBrain's skill and dream-cycle boundaries that captures the five signals and feeds them into both the GWL brain-training pipeline AND GBrain's skill-improvement workflow.\n\nThe clean way to say what v5 *is*: **assemble best-in-class agent-layer commodity beneath a GWL t2r brain, instrument the seam with quality-signal hooks, and use the result for daily work while the brain continues to deepen underneath.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. Current state recap\n\n**Status:** locked (consumed from STATE-OF-PROJECT-2026-05-13; tooling additions through 2026-05-14)\n\n### Brain layer (v4's territory) â€” substantially advanced\n\nWave 7 closed across three phases on 2026-05-08/09. Test gate moved 315 â†’ 374 â†’ 442 â†’ **498/498/0/0**. All scoped Wave-7 gaps closed: G-24 integrator-fuse, G-25 best-in-class skills, G-26 distillation losses, G-27 training-capture envelope, G-28 skill-evolution disabled-shell (gated off in Phase B), G-29 replay scheduler, G-31 drift-judge expert, G-32 adjudicator-tuple corpus. The Adjudicator-training-tuple corpus has been accumulating since 2026-05-08 â€” 5+ days of real capture data.\n\nPlasticity catalog landed 2026-05-07 (Session 6: 26 mutation IDs across 5 layers, despite v4 prose saying 17). Plasticity proposer skeleton landed 2026-05-07 (Session 7).\n\nPhase A foundations shipped 2026-05-07 (alpha-linear and alpha-lorentz pilot duo, SOULs at STRICT parity, per-agent page route, Phase B plan committed with `eng-1-9` u01-u03 unit slice locked). Linux smoke green; Windows smoke not yet confirmed.\n\nCURR-1 audit tool shipped 2026-05-07 (`audit-completeness.py`, 18 tests). The cp1252 / curly-quotes bug that ate the first audit pass is now fixed.\n\nPhase B first GLCI cadence ran 2026-05-07 evening (baseline 4/6 on both pilots, threshold 0.85).\n\n### Hardware â€” better than v4 anticipated\n\nHost migrated laptop â†’ **gwl-server** on 2026-05-12. Dell XPS 8960, i9-13900K (24c/32t), 32 GB DDR5 (single-DIMM punch-list item: matched second stick ~$80-100), RTX 4070 12 GB Ada, 1.8 TB OS NVMe + 1.9 TB workspace NVMe. Tailscale-served at `https://gwl.tailb49f65.ts.net`. **v4's INFRA-1 cloud-VM plan is moot** â€” the 12 GB VRAM on the 4070 caps Phase C at QLoRA on â‰¤8B students, which is exactly the design target for the local-distillation workstream.\n\nPhase C Step A scripts authored 2026-05-13 (paused per John's request, not blocking v5): `Install-Phase-C-Stack.ps1` + `smoke_qlora.py`. Decisions locked: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct + Miniconda + Python 3.12 + cu130 wheels + D:\\ for venv & weights.\n\n### Agent layer (v5's territory) â€” paper, but two enablers landed today\n\n`alpha` exists as an OpenClaw agent (Gemini-backed, 5 skills, sub-30s `hi` response after the 2026-04-30 recovery). `prime-coder` and `sigma` are operational. **Everything else in the agent registry is paper** â€” W/S/Math clusters are persona files, no spawned worker agent has run. The \"cluster operation\" v4 promised has not begun.\n\n`project-manager-kit` shipped 2026-05-07 and evolved substantially on 2026-05-13 during the anchor-coach pilot. Real-Intent Protocol (Parable of the Oranges) + Run-don't-narrate + use-claude-code skill + nine other T1/T2/T3 edits landed. Canonical relocated to `C:\\dev\\Project-Manager\\`; the Agentic-Home copy needs a one-way-sync resolution in S1.\n\n**Two enablers landed today (2026-05-14) that change v5 viability:**\n\n- **`expert-coder-Win11` skill shipped** â€” SKILL.md + 8 references + `Test-Win11Environment.ps1` (now bridge-aware after a real-host verification bug-fix this session) + CRITIQUE-PASS-1.md. All five mirror locations match by MD5 (canonical `C:\\dev\\Expert-Coder-Win11\\`, `skills-master\\`, `.claude\\skills\\`, `.gemini\\skills\\`, `~\\.openclaw\\skills\\`). Alpha and Prime Coder personas updated. Memory entry `skill_windows_coding` committed. Bootstrap auto-discovery confirmed (line 134 of Bootstrap-Agentic-Home.ps1 picks up any `skills-master\\<name>\\SKILL.md` without explicit reference).\n- **Coworkâ†”host PowerShell bridge is LIVE** (`mcp__Claude_Code_Bridge__run_command`). Destructive-op gating + admin-elevation flagging built in. Eliminates the paste-prompt failure mode the V5-RETRO flagged as the existential risk to execution time. Every v5 sprint task can now write *and run* host scripts directly.\n\nTogether these two reduce v5's \"what could go wrong at the Windows host\" risk class materially. Most Â§4 S1 tasks below assume the bridge is available; if it were not, S1 would need ~30% more elapsed time.\n\n### What this means for v5 sequencing\n\nThe brain layer has more than enough runway to start v0 right now without further brain work. The brain work *should continue in parallel* (the in-harness corpus that v0 produces is the input to the next brain-training cycle), but it does not gate v0. **The bottleneck is agent-layer work, not brain-layer work.**\n\n---\n\n## 3. The v0 / v1 / v2 spec table\n\n**Status:** locked (consumed from Retro Entry 15-18; walkthrough-confirmed with John 2026-05-14)\n\nThe critical path is one agent's ladder, not a phase-cluster arc. Specialization (cluster operation, W/S/Math agents) happens *after* v0 is in daily use.\n\n| Version | Agent layer | Brain layer | Sleep updates | Wallet (revised) | Gate to promote |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **v0** | OpenClaw runtime + GBrain (Tan's, v0.19+) on top with native `AGENTS.md` resolver at workspace root, t2r dispatcher as a brain-layer module GBrain calls. Best-in-class skill bundle (see Â§4). **Cowork-comparable for John's daily use.** Vehicle is the **`alpha-v0`** agent (shipped 2026-05-16 commits `6250cf5a` + `6482d06d`; separate tile and routing entry from the legacy `alpha` agent which still routes to the brain-research v2 orchestrator). In addition to alpha-v0 (the general-purpose vehicle), the v0 architecture supports **v0 specialists** â€” sibling agents with a different skill bundle + persona prompt + optional default project binding. Same dispatcher, same model defaults, same wallet. See Â§13 for the workstream cadence and Â§4 S2/S3 for the rollout. | t2r architecture: two sequential frontier calls per turn. Taste first (curate + sharp-query + skill/resource hint), then reasoning (execute against the curated context). No cognitive cells yet, no local LLMs. **Both halves top-tier frontier** â€” default **Gemini Pro both**, with **Anthropic Opus** available per-agent / per-turn for high-value escalation. Smart cheap/expensive routing is a v1+ capability, not v0. | Agent skills (success-rate scoring, retire low-performers â€” **human-in-loop through v0, no auto-retire**; aligns with GBrain's stated \"human-stays-in-the-loop\" philosophy), tools (usage stats, capability ranking), hooks (threshold tuning from outcomes). **No brain-side training in v0** â€” both halves are frontier-driven. | **$100/month per active agent** (soft alert at $80, hard cap enforced at the dispatcher with graceful-degrade + interactive cap-override). **Scope: measures t2r dispatcher calls only.** GBrain's internal LLM + embedding usage is a *separate* line item (see \"GBrain operational wallet\" note below the table). Cowork reference: $200/mo flat. **Revision triggers:** first soft-alert hit, OR end of S1 review, OR John reports cap is friction. Revision = `routing.json` commit + log entry in `logs/wallet-revisions.jsonl`. **Precedence:** raise the wallet, do not downgrade models. | **Per-workflow-type adoption matrix** (code / research / drafting / finance / trip-planning / project-management each tracked separately) replaces binary \"is John using it.\" Promotion to v1 = â‰¥3 workflow types showing â‰¥50% v0 adoption with stable signal quality. |\n| **v1** | Same as v0. | t2r with **cognitive cells** for taste-cell and reasoning-cell. Each cell still frontier-driven (no local LLMs). Cell substrate provides MoE-style expert routing inside each half. | Everything v0 sleeps **+** cognitive-cell config (gate residuals, expert routing inside cells, atlas concept reorganization). Still no LoRA â€” brain weights are not changing. | Same as v0 in expectation; cell substrate adds ~10% routing overhead amortized. | Cell substrate stably outperforms v0 on the same trust-ladder tasks for **2 weeks**. Cell-config sleep mutations demonstrably improving metric. |\n| **v2** | Same as v0. | v1 cognitive cells **+** local QLoRA-trained 8B student per cell (taste-student, reasoning-student) on the gwl-server 4070. **Gating function** decides per turn: local student vs frontier escalation. First neural plasticity. | Everything v1 sleeps **+** LoRA on the local students from the in-harness training corpus **+** gating-function calibration (when to escalate). | Wallet shrinks: gating diverts ~40-60% of turns to local students (~$0.001/turn) vs frontier (~$0.01-0.05/turn). Per-agent monthly burn target: **$300-700**. | Local students serve â‰¥40% of turns at â‰¥90% quality parity (measured via held-out frontier-judge). Gating function calibrated (escalates correctly on hard turns). |\n\nAfter v2 works on a single agent, GWL is in position to evaluate the full-brain rollout. After v0 works, **specialization can begin in parallel** with v1/v2 brain deepening â€” W/S/Math clusters as additional v0-shaped agents with specialty skill bundles.\n\n**GBrain operational wallet (separate from t2r wallet):** GBrain's own LLM and embedding-API usage during normal operation â€” dream cycle entity escalation, classifier fail-improve loop, retrieval embeddings â€” is tracked as a second wallet, distinct from v0's $100/mo t2r budget. Initial size: **$30/month** per active GBrain install. Audit task lands in S2 to right-size against real-use data. Confusion-avoidance rationale: a wallet-cap-hit in this line item should not be misread as v0 t2r failure; they are independently sized and tracked.\n\n### Resolution of seven drafting-time questions (walkthrough-locked 2026-05-14)\n\n1. **v0 wallet sizing.** **$100/month per active agent.** Soft alert at $80, hard cap enforced at the dispatcher with graceful-degrade behavior. Reference point: Cowork delivers comparable daily output at $200/mo flat, so v0 must demonstrate value at half that. Re-derive from observed envelope after one week of S1 data. **Precedence rule:** if $100 proves too tight for top-tier-both (Q2), *raise the wallet* â€” do not downgrade models. Per-skill cost attribution lands in S2.\n2. **Same vs different models for taste vs reasoning.** **Top-tier frontier on both halves.** Default: Gemini Pro both (cost math fits the $100 cap at ~30-300 turns/day). Anthropic Opus available per-agent and per-turn for high-value escalation. Smart cheap/expensive routing is genuinely a v1+ capability â€” v2's local-student-vs-frontier gating is exactly this kind of routing, and conflating it with v0 dilutes the t2r-architecture-purity demonstration that v0 exists to prove.\n3. **Naming collision.** Local `gbrain/` (and `gbrain-lite/`) renames to `gwl-runtime/` (and `gwl-runtime-lite/`). Tan's GBrain takes its native `gbrain/`. **Three-path disambiguation per Pass 2 B-11:** workspace-root, `$env:USERPROFILE\\gbrain\\` (the user-profile training-data root referenced in 30+ brain-code files), and Tan's GBrain install location are *three separate locations*. Spec memo `PATH-RENAME-design.md` precedes execution; lands S1 days 2-3.\n4. **Migration order.** **GBrain first.** Install OpenClaw + GBrain on gwl-server (S1 days 4-5), validate dream-cycle, then build t2r as a brain-layer module that GBrain's skill router invokes (S1 days 6-7). The t2r dispatcher is shaped to fit GBrain's `pre_turn` / `post_turn` hooks, not the reverse. **Schema extension to `routing.json` for per-half model fields (taste-model, reasoning-model) is load-bearing for the t2r dispatcher and must land in S1 day-8 regardless of cross-provider exercise.** Cross-provider wallet plumbing (Opus-escalation path) defers to S2.\n5. **First task / trust-ladder.** **No staged trust-ladder.** v0 ships as a general-purpose daily-use agent and John uses it for whatever comes up. **Success criterion is a per-workflow-type adoption matrix** â€” code, research, drafting, finance, trip-planning, project-management each tracked independently â€” not a binary \"is John using it.\" If 3+ types show â‰¥50% v0 adoption with stable signal quality, promotion to v1 is open. The 5-flavor signal capture (JSONL) runs task-agnostic so the matrix derives from real data, not survey questions. If v0 broadly fails across most types, the answer is *rethink the architecture* â€” see Â§11 Failure-mode contingencies.\n6. **v0 vehicle.** **The existing `alpha` agent becomes the v0 vehicle.** Alpha is already running, already in `routing.json`, already loads the v0 starter skill set, already has wallet plumbing. The persona-vs-reality gap (Retro Entry 6's \"Gap A â€” Capability\") is solvable by editing Alpha's persona to honestly describe what v0-Alpha does â€” **but the rewrite is its own design exercise**, captured in a separate design memo `PERSONA-DESIGN-Alpha-v0.md` authored in S1 day-1/2 before the conversion task. **Alpha's `routing.json` entry must explicitly upgrade to top-tier model per half per Q2** â€” current entry is Gemini Flash (post-2026-04-30 recovery); v0 requires Gemini Pro both halves as default, Opus available per-turn. `alpha-linear` and `alpha-lorentz` (the brain-research pilot duo) stay on their separate track â€” they're SOUL-twin pilots for Phase B brain work, not the daily-use vehicle.\n7. **Cadence.** **Sprints and Brain Waves run in parallel.** Sprint S1, S2, â€¦ (~2-week units) for agent-layer v0/v1/v2 work, anchored to deliverables. Brain Wave 8+ for brain-side work (corpus-fed distillation, plasticity activation, drift refinement), anchored to test-gate jumps. Different work shapes deserve different cadences; forcing both into one taxonomy mislabels what's being tracked.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The v0 build plan â€” concrete, dated, decomposed\n\n**Status:** master (incorporating Pass 1 + Pass 2 amendments)\n\n### Sprint S1 â€” Foundation (target 2026-05-15 through 2026-06-04, 3 weeks)\n\n3 weeks: weeks 1-2 deliverables (5/15-5/29), week 3 catch-up + S2 prep (5/30-6/4). Slack is a feature â€” week 3 absorbs the unknowns concentrated in the rename + GBrain install days. If everything green by end of week 2, week 3 starts S2 early; that's a win, not lost time.\n\n| Day | Task | Verify gate |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5/15 | (a) Author `PERSONA-DESIGN-Alpha-v0.md` design memo â€” drops cluster-dispatch promises, defines what v0-Alpha actually does as a t2r general-purpose daily-use agent, decides what survives from current persona vs what's redesigned. (b) Run Bootstrap-Agentic-Home.ps1 on host to propagate `expert-coder-Win11`. (c) Mirror `skills-master\\expert-coder-Win11\\` â†’ canonical `C:\\dev\\Expert-Coder-Win11\\`. *Note: (b) and (c) already complete as of session that promoted this plan; verify state matches and check off.* | Design memo committed. `openclaw skills info expert-coder-Win11` returns the skill from a *fresh* PS session. `Test-Path C:\\dev\\Expert-Coder-Win11\\SKILL.md` is True. |\n| 5/16 | Sync `C:\\dev\\Project-Manager\\` (canonical) â†’ `Agentic-Home\\skills-master\\project-manager\\` (direction is canonical â†’ propagation, not bidirectional). Re-run Bootstrap to propagate to runtime mirrors. | `diff -r` between source and propagated copy returns empty. `openclaw skills info project-manager` returns the skill. |\n| 5/16-5/17 | **B-11: Namespace rename (multi-location).** Author `PATH-RENAME-design.md` spec (half-day). Then execute the three-location disambiguation: (a) `C:\\dev\\Agentic-Home\\gbrain\\` â†’ `C:\\dev\\Agentic-Home\\gwl-runtime\\` (workspace-root runtime state); (b) `Move-Item C:\\Users\\john\\gbrain C:\\Users\\john\\gwl-corpus -Verbose` (brain training-data root; preserves 5+ days of Wave-7 corpus); (c) leave a hole for Tan's GBrain to claim workspace-root `gbrain/` post-install. Scripted find-and-replace `'gbrain'` â†’ `'gwl-corpus'` across the brain tree (30+ files); update CLAUDE.md \"Don't-touch list\" + `.gitignore` + memory entries. **Test gate stays green post-rename.** | (a) Brain test suite still 498/498/0/0 post-rename. (b) `grep -r \"['\\\"]gbrain['\\\"]\" sandbox-lab-gui